nodecode-alice-voice is a rewrite of bablobanov's bablobanov/hermes-alice-voice:

  https://github.com/bablobanov/hermes-alice-voice
  commit e31f2c8e65bb861c6806bd19e8f57a1e89726450
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Ilya Balobanov (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's one working part, bridge.py, as cell.lisp --
the same bearer-in-the-path secret and 64-hex rule, the same order of gates
(running, path, protocol version, skill id, account, message ids), the same
controller words answered locally, the same in-memory deduplication of the
last 32 message ids, and the same ack that claims scheduling and not
delivery. The words it speaks are the original's, with the destination
renamed from Telegram to Nodecode.

What was left out, and why: the plugin's own HTTP server (the cell answers on
the gateway's own port as a route, so there is no port setting and no thread
to close); the ALICE_WEBHOOK_SECRET environment variable (the secret is a
field of the cell's section); the Hermes Telegram handler registration and its
unload callback (the route is registered and taken back by the cell
declaration); the core estop pause check (a Nodecode turn queues behind a
running one instead of being refused); and the Hermes-specific session key,
replaced by the id of the Nodecode session the requests land in. The plugin's
Python tests were not ported; the cell has its own slice.
